---
title: Custom Strategies
description: Replace or extend built-in drop zone strategies, or add entirely new container modes.
---

# Custom Strategies

You can replace or extend the built-in `'sortable'` and `'target'` strategies, or add entirely new modes by implementing the `ContainerStrategy` interface.

## Built-in strategies

| Mode | Strategy class | Description |
|------|---------------|-------------|
| `'sortable'` | `SortableContainerStrategy` | Position-based drop zones with insert previews. Supports `vertical`, `horizontal`, and `grid` directions |
| `'target'` | `TargetContainerStrategy` | Single drop zone covering the whole container, no previews |

## Passing a strategy factory

The built-in strategies receive a `StrategyContext` (`{ state, droppablesById }`) in their constructor. Since the context is created inside `DndController`, pass a **factory function** when your strategy needs it — the controller will call it with the internal context:

```ts
import {
    DndController,
    SortableContainerStrategy,
    type ContainerStrategy,
    type StrategyFactory,
    type StrategyContext,
    type DropZone,
    type DndMode,
    type Droppable,
} from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';

class MasonryStrategy extends SortableContainerStrategy {
    override readonly mode: DndMode = 'masonry'

    override calculateDropZones(droppable: Droppable, session): DropZone[] {
        const slots = droppable.getSortedSlots()
        const direction = droppable.direction
        // your masonry-specific zone calculation using droppable.element, slots, direction, etc.
        return []
    }
}

const masonryFactory: StrategyFactory = (ctx) => new MasonryStrategy(ctx)

const controller = new DndController({
    strategies: [masonryFactory]
})
```

Then use the custom mode on a droppable:

```svelte
<DndDroppable mode="masonry" id="board">
    ...
</DndDroppable>
```

## Passing a plain instance

If your strategy doesn't need the controller's context (i.e. it implements `ContainerStrategy` directly without extending a built-in class), you can pass a plain instance:

```ts
const controller = new DndController({
    strategies: [new SimpleStrategy()]
})
```

## Overriding built-in strategies

Passing a factory with `mode: 'sortable'` or `mode: 'target'` **replaces** the built-in behaviour for that mode globally:

```ts
class MySortableStrategy extends SortableContainerStrategy {
    override readonly mode: DndMode = 'sortable'

    override calculateDropZones(droppable: Droppable, session): DropZone[] {
        // replaces built-in sortable zone calculation
        return super.calculateDropZones(droppable, session)
    }
}

const controller = new DndController({
    strategies: [(ctx) => new MySortableStrategy(ctx)]
})
```

## Types

```ts
import type { Droppable, DndState, DragSession, AnimationStep } from '@horuse/svelte-dnd';

interface ContainerStrategy {
    readonly mode: DndMode;
    calculateDropZones(droppable: Droppable, session: DragSession | null): DropZone[];
    getTranslations(droppable: Droppable, session: DragSession): Map<string, { x: number; y: number }>;
    getDropAnimation(session: DragSession, targetZone: DropZone): AnimationStep;
    getReturnAnimation(session: DragSession): AnimationStep;
}

interface StrategyContext {
    state: DndState;
    droppablesById: Map<string, Droppable>;
}

type StrategyFactory = (ctx: StrategyContext) => ContainerStrategy;
```

